Yes — that’s the distinction. The brand shouldn’t explain the product. The product should teach people what the brand means.

I’d name it Veyr.

Short, sharp, masculine without trying too hard. It looks like it could already be a European design house, but it doesn’t lock you into hair, grooming, fashion, or hardware.

Over time, Veyr comes to mean: quiet objects with obsessive design — things that almost disappear, but reward attention.

The clip could simply be Veyr No. 01 or Veyr I. No “hair clamp” product-name nonsense on the object itself.

And the mark should be nearly invisible: a tiny V monogram, same material, maybe a different surface finish rather than a different color. Matte body / satin V, or black PVD / slightly darker black V. You only catch it when light moves across it.

That gives you the Gucci effect you’re talking about: Veyr has no inherent meaning. We earn the meaning.

A few others in exactly that lane that I think have potential: Sable, Veyr, Orsen, Kova, Vale, Renn, Calder, Senn, Arlo, Slate.

But for this particular object, my board would be:

VEY R — strongest invented-house feel
SABLE — strongest existing-word luxury feel
SLATE — strongest modern industrial-design feel

I’d build Veyr. It has somewhere to go.